<TopicPage slug="subjects/logistics-supply-chain/foundational/supply-chain-concepts-and-metrics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    A supply chain is every company and process standing between raw material in the ground and a
    product in a customer's hands — mines, mills, component makers, assemblers, distributors,
    carriers, retailers. Supply chain management is the discipline of coordinating all of it, which
    is genuinely strange, because no one owns the chain. Your phone's assembler does not own its
    chip supplier, which does not own the silicon refiner, which does not own the retailer; they
    are separate firms with separate bosses, separate accounting and often conflicting incentives.
    The field's whole problem is cooperation across boundaries that no contract can fully bridge.
  </p>
  <p>
    The classic way to see through the complexity is to stop looking at the boxes (companies,
    factories, warehouses) and look at the <strong>flows</strong> connecting them. Three of them
    run through every chain. <em>Product</em> flows downstream: ore becomes steel becomes parts
    becomes product becomes delivery. <em>Money</em> flows upstream: the customer's payment is
    split into everyone's revenue on the way back. <em>Information</em> flows both ways — orders
    and forecasts downstream-to-upstream, availability, lead times and shipment status
    upstream-to-downstream. Nearly every famous supply chain failure turns out to be a flow
    problem, not a box problem: information that was late, distorted or absent, causing product to
    pile up in the wrong place or vanish from the right one. The empty toilet-paper shelves of
    2020 were not a shortage of pulp; they were an information cascade, as every link in the chain
    simultaneously ordered extra against the same rumour of panic buying.
  </p>
  <p>
    A second clarifying lens: the chain has only one <strong>customer</strong>. Every upstream
    participant — however many invoices it sends — ultimately serves the person who buys the
    finished product, and final demand is the only true signal. Everything else upstream is
    derived from it. That is why the discipline says the chain exists downstream, and why the
    hardest coordination problems start with the fact that most companies can't see final demand
    at all: the semiconductor plant sees orders from assemblers, who see orders from retailers,
    who see actual shoppers — and each layer in between injects its own noise.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="flows, trade-offs and the SCOR vocabulary" widget="equationStep" config={chainCfg} />

  <h2>The end-to-end chain: flows, trade-offs and the bullwhip</h2>
  <p>
    Managed well, the chain is invisible — you click, the item arrives. Managed badly it is empty
    shelves, idled factories, expediting fees and a very loud news cycle. The design tension that
    governs everything is the <strong>trade-off triangle</strong>: service level (how reliably
    demand is met, on time and in full), inventory (how much capital and space is tied up in
    stock) and cost (the total expense of sourcing, making and moving). You can perform brilliantly
    on any two; the third collects the bill. A chain can promise near-perfect availability with
    minimal inventory only by paying for speed — air freight, duplicate suppliers, buffer capacity.
    Every decision from network design to the size of a purchase order lives inside this triangle,
    and most supply chain arguments are really arguments about which corner to sacrifice.
  </p>
  <p>
    The most famous demonstration of why coordination is hard is the <strong>bullwhip
    effect</strong>. A retailer sees shopper demand wiggle slightly — say, a case of soup selling
    unusually well one week. Uncertain whether it is a trend, and unwilling to lose sales, the
    retailer orders up, with a buffer. The wholesaler sees a bigger order jump and, applying its
    own safety margin, orders up harder from the factory. The factory sees what looks like a boom,
    adds capacity and raw material orders, and the suppliers of suppliers amplify again. Small
    wobbles at the shelf become violent swings upstream — the crack of a whip — so factories and
    mines live through booms and busts that the actual shopper never caused. It was first
    formalised from Procter &amp; Gamble's observation that diaper orders swung wildly even though
    babies consume diapers at an almost perfectly steady rate. The causes are systematic: order
    batching, long lead times, price promotions that encourage forward-buying, and each tier
    forecasting from the tier below it instead of from true demand. The fixes are informational —
    share point-of-sale data upstream (the essence of retailer–supplier collaboration), cut batch
    sizes, smooth prices, shorten lead times — which is why visibility is treated as the
    discipline's most valuable commodity.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="each stage orders more wildly than the last" widget="plotter" config={bullwhipCfg} />
  <p>
    Underneath the flows sits a strategic choice that Marshall Fisher (1997) made canonical:
    match the chain to the product. <strong>Functional products</strong> — detergent, staple
    foods, commodity parts — have predictable demand and compete on price; they deserve
    <em>efficient</em> chains that squeeze cost, run full trucks and hold lean inventory.
    <strong>Innovative products</strong> — fashion, electronics, anything with a short season and
    volatile demand — deserve <em>responsive</em> chains that buy speed and flexibility even at
    higher cost, because the expensive failure is not the air freight bill but the markdown bin
    full of last season's stock. Mismatched pairs are where supply chains visibly fail: the
    efficient chain serving fashion produces chronic stockouts of the hits and mountains of the
    misses; the responsive chain serving detergent simply costs more than the competition can
    match.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that the bullwhip means customers are unpredictable. Final demand is often the steadiest thing in the chain — it is the <em>ordering behaviour</em> of intermediate firms, each protecting itself against uncertainty, that manufactures the wild swings. When executives blame "erratic consumer demand" for inventory gluts, the discipline's answer is: look upstream at the amplification your own tiers created.</p>

  <h2>SCOR and the metrics that make chains discussable</h2>
  <p>
    A chain that spans companies cannot be managed by goodwill; it needs a shared vocabulary and
    agreed numbers. The <strong>SCOR model</strong> (Supply Chain Operations Reference model,
    stewarded by ASCM, the Association for Supply Chain Management) supplies both. Its process
    vocabulary organises all supply chain work into <strong>Plan</strong> (balance demand and
    supply), <strong>Source</strong> (procure goods and services), <strong>Make</strong>
    (transform), <strong>Deliver</strong> (order management, warehousing, transportation),
    <strong>Return</strong> (reverse logistics — the returns that now define e-commerce) and
    <strong>Enable</strong> (the rules, data and systems the other processes run on; newer SCOR
    versions add <em>Orchestrate</em> for strategy and workforce). The value is precision: "we
    need to improve logistics" becomes "our Deliver process is weak on responsiveness", and two
    companies that have never worked together can still audit each other in the same terms.
  </p>
  <p>
    SCOR's metrics cluster into five attribute families. <strong>Reliability</strong> is measured
    by the <em>perfect order</em> — delivered complete, on time, undamaged, with correct
    documentation — and its rate is the single most customer-visible number in the field, because
    a 97 per cent score means three orders in a hundred failed at somebody's expense.
    <strong>Responsiveness</strong> is the order fulfilment cycle time: order placed to delivery.
    <strong>Agility</strong> measures how fast the chain can scale up for an unexpected surge and
    how long it can sustain a downturn. <strong>Cost</strong> is tracked as total supply chain
    management cost and cost of goods sold. <strong>Asset management efficiency</strong> asks how
    hard the chain's capital works, and its two headline ratios deserve unpacking.
    <em>Inventory turns</em> — cost of goods sold divided by average inventory — compresses the
    whole inventory-versus-availability tension into one comparable figure: a grocer turning stock
    20 times a year holds about 18 days of sales; a heavy-equipment maker turning 2 times holds
    six months. <em>Cash-to-cash cycle time</em> measures the days between paying suppliers and
    being paid by customers — inventory days plus receivable days minus payable days — the number
    that reveals whether growth consumes cash or releases it. In 2020, the firms that weathered
    disruption best were not those with the lowest cash-to-cash but those that could see and flex
    fastest — which is why agility metrics joined the classic efficiency ones.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked example ties the metrics together. Two distributors both sell 10 million units a
    year. Distributor A holds 40 million in average inventory (turns of 2.5, assuming cost basis)
    and a cash-to-cash of 60 days; Distributor B, having invested in shared forecasts with its key
    supplier, holds 16 million (turns of 6) with cash-to-cash of 25 days — at a slightly higher
    transport cost from more frequent, smaller deliveries. B's balance sheet can grow twice as
    fast on the same capital. The chain did not get cheaper; it got <em>faster</em>, and in supply
    chains speed is money.
  </p>

  <h2>Customer and supplier segments: no chain is one-size-fits-all</h2>
  <p>
    Because service is expensive, mature supply chains do not serve everyone identically — they
    <strong>segment</strong>. Customer segmentation groups customers by what service they need and
    what they are worth, so scarce capacity goes where it pays. The common shape: customers ranked
    by profitability and volume (the classic ABC analysis), then assigned differentiated promises —
    key accounts get dedicated capacity, priority allocation in shortages and custom delivery
    windows; the mid-tier gets standard service; tiny, high-cost-to-serve accounts may be moved to
    less expensive channels. The analytical work is honest cost-to-serve accounting: a small
    account that orders erratically, demands split shipments and pays in 90 days can consume far
    more chain resources than its margin funds — a fact invisible until the chain is segmented.
  </p>
  <p>
    Supplier segmentation works from the buyer's side, and its standard tool is the
    <strong>Kraljic matrix</strong> (Peter Kraljic, 1983), which plots each purchased item by
    profit impact and supply risk. Four quadrants, four strategies. <em>Strategic</em> items (high
    impact, high risk — the custom chips in a car maker's BOM) get partnership: joint development,
    long-term contracts, executive attention. <em>Leverage</em> items (high impact, low risk —
    commodity packaging) get competition: market testing, aggressive negotiation, multiple bidders.
    <em>Bottleneck</em> items (low spend, high risk — a sole-source specialty chemical) get risk
    management: buffer stock, qualified alternates, contract security. <em>Non-critical</em> items
    (office supplies) get efficiency: catalogues, automation, and as little managerial attention
    as possible. The matrix's enduring insight is that procurement effort is itself a scarce
    resource and should be spent where the risk-and-value profile justifies it — the corporate
    world is full of teams negotiating hard over leverage items while strategic suppliers drift
    toward failure unwatched.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three refinements carry you toward intermediate work. First, <strong>decoupling points</strong>:
    every chain must choose where to hold its inventory of generic product and where final
    customer demand "enters" the process — make-to-stock (push finished goods to a warehouse,
    as with groceries), assemble-to-order (hold modules, configure at the last step, as with
    laptops), or make-to-order (begin production on the order itself, as with aircraft). The
    postponement principle — keep product generic as long as possible, differentiate late —
    explains why a global apparel brand ships undyed fabric and colours it after early sales data
    arrives. Second, <strong>inventory has types</strong> with different economics: cycle stock
    (the portion that turns over with batch sizes), safety stock (the buffer against forecast
    error and lead-time variability, sized statistically against a target service level),
    pipeline stock (in transit — roughly global commerce holds weeks of sales on ships), and
    anticipation stock (built ahead of predictable seasonal peaks). Third, the current research
    frontier: resilience economics. Decades of optimisation pushed chains toward lowest cost and
    just-in-time inventory, and the pandemic, the Suez blockage and tariff shocks revealed that
    resilience was an unpriced insurance policy. The field now quantifies the trade explicitly —
    dual sourcing, nearshoring, and strategic stock as purchased option value against disruption —
    which is, in the end, the trade-off triangle wearing a new face: you still choose two of
    service, cost and inventory; resilience is the price of keeping the promise under stress.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> ASCM CSCP Exam Content Manual; Simchi-Levi et al., <em>Designing and Managing the Supply Chain</em>; Fisher, "What Is the Right Supply Chain for Your Product?" (1997); Kraljic, "Purchasing Must Become Supply Management" (1983).</p>
